      <div class=3D"content__content">
        <p><span style=3D"font-size: 28px; font-family: cursive;">Deconstru=
cting the Year=E2=80=99s Most Damaging API Attacks, Breaches, and Exposures=
</span></p>

<p>The global API management market is projected to generate around <a href=
=3D"https://scoop.market.us/api-management-statistics/" target=3D"_blank" d=
ata-cmp-ab=3D"2" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer">$9.7 billion</a> in revenue th=
is year (depending on the source, estimates range from $7 billion to $ 12 b=
illion), underscoring that APIs are now the connective tissue of modern bus=
iness=E2=80=94and a <a href=3D"https://equixly.com/blog/2024/04/24/api-atta=
ck-surface/" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer">growing attack s=
urface</a>.</p>

<p>This last point is emphatically confirmed by this year=E2=80=99s most da=
maging API security incidents and exposures:</p>

<ul>
  <li>Stripe=E2=80=99s legacy API attack (merchant-targeted skimming campai=
gn)</li>
  <li>McDonald=E2=80=99s / Paradox.ai data incident</li>
  <li>Intel employees=E2=80=99 data exposure</li>
  <li>Volkswagen=E2=80=99s multiple API flaw</li>
  <li>Azure AD users=E2=80=99 sensitive information exposure</li>
</ul>

<p>You will learn the technical root causes and the key lessons from these =
incidents.</p>

<p>Security professionals and developers can approach these five API incide=
nts as case studies for learning and improvement. Chronicles of how exploit=
ing API logic flaws, technical vulnerabilities, and security oversights res=
ult in operational disruptions with lasting business fallout. Signs that pr=
eventing an API security incident must be the cornerstone of any security s=
trategy.</p>

<p><strong>Note</strong>: Not all these incidents involved confirmed malici=
ous exploitation. Some were researcher-disclosed exposures, included here b=
ecause they highlight the same systemic API security weaknesses attackers r=
outinely exploit.</p>

<h2 id=3D"1-stripes-legacy-api-the-ghost-of-technical-debt">1. Stripe=E2=80=
=99s Legacy API: The Ghost of Technical Debt</h2>

<p><img src=3D"https://equixly.com/assets/blog/5-defining-api-incidents-202=
5/stripe_credit-card.svg" alt=3D"Stripe Credit Card Skimming" data-cmp-info=
=3D"10"></p>

<p>This attack did not compromise Stripe=E2=80=99s core, current infrastruc=
ture. Instead, it was a web-skimming campaign against merchants that abused=
 a deprecated, legacy Stripe API endpoint. This forgotten doorway became a =
prime vector for a large-scale card-testing and skimming operation, illustr=
ating the dangers of zombie and <a href=3D"https://equixly.com/blog/2024/08=
/05/shadow-api/" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer">shadow APIs<=
/a>.</p>

<h3 id=3D"the-anatomy-of-the-attack">The Anatomy of the Attack</h3>
<p>Attackers found a legacy endpoint (<span style=3D"font-family: cursive;"=
>/v1/sources</span>) that was still connected to backend payment validation=
 systems. This endpoint lacked the security controls of Stripe=E2=80=99s mo=
dern APIs, such as advanced rate limiting and fraud detection algorithms.</=
p>

<p>They used stolen card lists and bots to flood the API with small transac=
tion requests. Each request provided valuable information, regardless of wh=
ether it was successful or unsuccessful. A successful transaction confirmed=
 the validity of a card, marking it for fraudulent use or sale on the dark =
web.</p>

<p>The core problem here was a combination of Improper Asset Management and=
 <a href=3D"https://equixly.com/blog/2023/12/29/broken-authentication-api-k=
eys-and-how-to-lose-$26m-in-crypto/" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener nore=
ferrer">Broken Authentication</a> vulnerabilities. The legacy endpoint lack=
ed the stringent authentication of Stripe=E2=80=99s production APIs. It was=
 a remnant of older architecture=E2=80=94poorly documented and overlooked i=
n security audits.</p>

<p>This is not unusual. On average, organizations test only <a href=3D"http=
s://www.traceable.ai/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/2025-Global-State-of-API-Se=
curity.pdf" target=3D"_blank" data-cmp-ab=3D"2" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer"=
>38% of their APIs</a> for vulnerabilities, leaving legacy or shadow APIs d=
angerously exposed.</p>

<p>Since this API endpoint was not part of the official inventory, Stripe d=
idn=E2=80=99t subject it to the same rigorous testing and monitoring as oth=
er APIs. That allowed the attackers=E2=80=99 high-frequency, low-value requ=
ests to fly under the radar of standard security alerts for an extended per=
iod.</p>

